    <name>Title</name>
    <text>System and hardware</text>
    <x>15</x>
    <y>10</y>
    <width>500</width>
    <height>30</height>
    <font>
      <font name="Header 2" family="Liberation Sans" style="BOLD" size="18.0">
      </font>
    </font>
  </widget>
  <widget type="textupdate" version="2.0.0">
    <name>Version</name>
    <pv_name>$(P):SYS:VERSION</pv_name>
    <x>840</x>
    <y>10</y>
    <width>310</width>
    <height>28</height>
    <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
    <vertical_alignment>1</vertical_alignment>
  </widget>
  <widget type="group" version="3.0.0">
    <name>Time</name>
    <x>12</x>
    <y>52</y>
    <width>566</width>
    <height>141</height>
    <font>
      <font name="Header 3" family="Liberation Sans" style="BOLD" size="16.0">
      </font>
    </font>
    <widget type="label" version="2.0.0">
      <name>Title</name>
      <text>Time source</text>
      <width>200</width>
      <height>24</height>
    </widget>
    <widget type="combo" version="2.0.0">
      <name>Source</name>
      <pv_name>$(P):SYS:TIME_SOURCE</pv_name>
      <y>30</y>
      <width>190</width>
      <height>32</height>
      <tooltip>$(P):SYS:TIME_SOURCE (TIME_SOURCE)

Selects which clock the timestamps are built from:
  System time  - the Linux system clock of the Zynq board.
  White Rabbit - the WR timestamp latched by the FPGA, which is the one to
                 use for correlating with the rest of the accelerator.

Applies to the current time below and to the interlock event times.</tooltip>
      <border_alarm_sensitive>false</border_alarm_sensitive>
      <items>
        <item>System time</item>
        <item>White Rabbit</item>
      </items>
    </widget>
    <widget type="label" version="2.0.0">
      <name>SourceLabel</name>
      <text>Time source</text>
      <y>64</y>
      <width>190</width>
      <height>22</height>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
    </widget>
    <widget type="textupdate" version="2.0.0">
      <name>Value</name>
      <pv_name>$(P):TIME_S</pv_name>
      <x>200</x>
      <y>30</y>
      <width>320</width>
      <height>32</height>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
      <vertical_alignment>1</vertical_alignment>
      <tooltip>$(P):TIME_S (TIME_S)

Current timestamp from the selected time source, formatted as
YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS.nnnnnnnnn. The White Rabbit time already carries UTC+8,
the driver subtracts 28800 s to recover UTC.</tooltip>
      <border_alarm_sensitive>false</border_alarm_sensitive>
    </widget>
    <widget type="label" version="2.0.0">
      <name>ValueLabel</name>
      <text>Current time</text>
      <x>200</x>
      <y>62</y>
      <width>320</width>
      <height>22</height>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
    </widget>
  </widget>
  <widget type="group" version="3.0.0">
    <name>WR</name>
    <x>597</x>
    <y>52</y>
    <width>566</width>
    <height>141</height>
    <font>
      <font name="Header 3" family="Liberation Sans" style="BOLD" size="16.0">
      </font>
    </font>
    <widget type="label" version="2.0.0">
      <name>Title</name>
      <text>White Rabbit hardware</text>
      <width>250</width>
      <height>24</height>
    </widget>
    <widget type="textentry" version="3.0.0">
      <name>Offset</name>
      <pv_name>$(P):SYS:WB_TM_OFS</pv_name>
      <y>30</y>
      <width>170</width>
      <height>32</height>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
      <tooltip>$(P):SYS:WB_TM_OFS (MISC_WB_TM_OFS)

Offset in seconds added to the White Rabbit timestamp received from the FPGA
before it is published. Use it to compensate a fixed offset between the WR
network time and the time reference used by the rest of the facility.
Default 162 s.</tooltip>
      <border_alarm_sensitive>false</border_alarm_sensitive>
    </widget>
    <widget type="combo" version="2.0.0">
      <name>IDMode</name>
      <pv_name>$(P):SYS:ID_MODE</pv_name>
      <x>190</x>
      <y>30</y>
      <width>170</width>
      <height>32</height>
      <tooltip>$(P):SYS:ID_MODE (MISC_ID_MODE)

Selects how the FPGA stamps the data packets it writes into BRAM:
  Incremental ID  - each packet gets a locally incremented identifier.
  White Rabbit time - each packet is stamped with the White Rabbit time, so
                      the samples can be placed on the accelerator time
                      axis.</tooltip>
      <border_alarm_sensitive>false</border_alarm_sensitive>
      <items>
        <item>Incremental ID</item>
        <item>White Rabbit time</item>
      </items>
    </widget>
    <widget type="label" version="2.0.0">
      <name>OffsetLabel</name>
      <text>WR time offset</text>
      <y>64</y>
      <width>170</width>
      <height>22</height>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
    </widget>
    <widget type="label" version="2.0.0">
      <name>IDModeLabel</name>
      <text>FPGA data ID mode</text>
      <x>190</x>
      <y>64</y>
      <width>170</width>
      <height>22</height>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
    </widget>
  </widget>
  <widget type="group" version="3.0.0">
    <name>Temperature</name>
    <x>12</x>
    <y>212</y>
    <width>566</width>
    <height>131</height>
    <font>
      <font name="Header 3" family="Liberation Sans" style="BOLD" size="16.0">
      </font>
    </font>
    <widget type="label" version="2.0.0">
      <name>Title</name>
      <text>Temperature (alarm at 90 C)</text>
      <width>300</width>
      <height>24</height>
    </widget>
    <widget type="textupdate" version="2.0.0">
      <name>PS</name>
      <pv_name>$(P):ZynqMP:PS:Temp</pv_name>
      <y>30</y>
      <width>170</width>
      <height>32</height>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
      <vertical_alignment>1</vertical_alignment>
      <tooltip>$(P):ZynqMP:PS:Temp (PS_TEMP)

Junction temperature of the Zynq UltraScale+ processing system, read from the
on-chip AMS sensor by the monitor driver once a second. High alarm at 75 C,
high-high alarm at 85 C.</tooltip>
    </widget>
    <widget type="textupdate" version="2.0.0">
      <name>PL</name>
      <pv_name>$(P):ZynqMP:PL:Temp</pv_name>
      <x>190</x>
      <y>30</y>
      <width>170</width>
      <height>32</height>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
      <vertical_alignment>1</vertical_alignment>
      <tooltip>$(P):ZynqMP:PL:Temp (PL_TEMP)

Junction temperature of the Zynq UltraScale+ programmable logic, read from
the on-chip AMS sensor by the monitor driver once a second. High alarm at
75 C, high-high alarm at 85 C. A rising PL temperature while the beam is on
usually means the FPGA logic is dissipating more than expected.</tooltip>
    </widget>
    <widget type="label" version="2.0.0">
      <name>PSLabel</name>
      <text>PS junction (C)</text>
      <y>64</y>
      <width>170</width>
      <height>22</height>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
    </widget>
    <widget type="label" version="2.0.0">
      <name>PLLabel</name>
      <text>PL junction (C)</text>
      <x>190</x>
      <y>64</y>
      <width>170</width>
      <height>22</height>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
    </widget>
  </widget>
  <widget type="group" version="3.0.0">
    <name>Power</name>
    <x>597</x>
    <y>212</y>
    <width>566</width>
    <height>131</height>
    <font>
      <font name="Header 3" family="Liberation Sans" style="BOLD" size="16.0">
      </font>
    </font>
    <widget type="label" version="2.0.0">
      <name>Title</name>
      <text>Power rails</text>
      <width>200</width>
      <height>24</height>
    </widget>
    <widget type="textupdate" version="2.0.0">
      <name>V1</name>
      <pv_name>$(P):ZynqMP:VCCINT</pv_name>
      <y>30</y>
      <width>160</width>
      <height>32</height>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
      <vertical_alignment>1</vertical_alignment>
      <tooltip>$(P):ZynqMP:VCCINT (VCCINT)

Core supply of the Zynq UltraScale+ programmable logic, read from the on-chip
AMS sensor. Nominal 0.85 V, low-low alarm at 0.55 V, high alarm at 1.50 V.</tooltip>
      <border_alarm_sensitive>false</border_alarm_sensitive>
    </widget>
    <widget type="textupdate" version="2.0.0">
      <name>V2</name>
      <pv_name>$(P):ZynqMP:VCCAUX</pv_name>
      <x>180</x>
      <y>30</y>
      <width>160</width>
      <height>32</height>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
      <vertical_alignment>1</vertical_alignment>
      <tooltip>$(P):ZynqMP:VCCAUX (VCCAUX)

Auxiliary supply of the Zynq UltraScale+, read from the on-chip AMS sensor.
Nominal 1.80 V, low-low alarm at 1.70 V, high alarm at 2.00 V.</tooltip>
      <border_alarm_sensitive>false</border_alarm_sensitive>
    </widget>
    <widget type="textupdate" version="2.0.0">
      <name>V3</name>
      <pv_name>$(P):ZynqMP:VCCBRAM</pv_name>
      <x>360</x>
      <y>30</y>
      <width>160</width>
      <height>32</height>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
      <vertical_alignment>1</vertical_alignment>
      <tooltip>$(P):ZynqMP:VCCBRAM (VCCBRAM)

Block RAM supply of the Zynq UltraScale+, read from the on-chip AMS sensor.
Nominal 0.85 V, low-low alarm at 0.80 V, high alarm at 0.90 V.</tooltip>
      <border_alarm_sensitive>false</border_alarm_sensitive>
    </widget>
    <widget type="label" version="2.0.0">
      <name>V1Label</name>
      <text>VCCINT (V)</text>
      <y>64</y>
      <width>160</width>
      <height>22</height>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
    </widget>
    <widget type="label" version="2.0.0">
      <name>V2Label</name>
      <text>VCCAUX (V)</text>
      <x>180</x>
      <y>64</y>
      <width>160</width>
      <height>22</height>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
    </widget>
    <widget type="label" version="2.0.0">
      <name>V3Label</name>
      <text>VCCBRAM (V)</text>
      <x>360</x>
      <y>64</y>
      <width>160</width>
      <height>22</height>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
    </widget>
  </widget>
  <widget type="group" version="3.0.0">
    <name>DAC</name>
    <x>12</x>
    <y>362</y>
    <width>1151</width>
    <height>131</height>
    <font>
      <font name="Header 3" family="Liberation Sans" style="BOLD" size="16.0">
      </font>
    </font>
    <widget type="label" version="2.0.0">
      <name>Title</name>
      <text>DAC80508 reference hardware</text>
      <width>300</width>
      <height>24</height>
    </widget>
    <widget type="textupdate" version="2.0.0">
      <name>ID</name>
      <pv_name>$(P):SYS:DAC_DEV_ID</pv_name>
      <y>30</y>
      <width>180</width>
      <height>32</height>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
      <vertical_alignment>1</vertical_alignment>
      <tooltip>$(P):SYS:DAC_DEV_ID (DAC_DEV_ID)

DEVICE ID read back from the TI DAC80508 over the AXI Quad SPI link. A
non-zero value confirms the DAC responds; if it stays at zero the SPI link or
the bitstream address is wrong.</tooltip>
      <border_alarm_sensitive>false</border_alarm_sensitive>
    </widget>
    <widget type="action_button" version="3.0.0">
      <name>Reset</name>
      <actions>
        <action type="write_pv">
          <description>Write PV</description>
          <pv_name>$(pv_name)</pv_name>
          <value>1</value>
        </action>
      </actions>
      <pv_name>$(P):SYS:DAC_RESET</pv_name>
      <text>Reset DAC</text>
      <x>200</x>
      <y>30</y>
      <width>180</width>
      <height>32</height>
      <tooltip>$(actions)

Sends a software reset to the DAC80508 and then re-applies the current
reference setpoints of all six channels, so the comparator thresholds are
restored without reloading from autosave.</tooltip>
      <border_alarm_sensitive>false</border_alarm_sensitive>
    </widget>
    <widget type="label" version="2.0.0">
      <name>IDLabel</name>
      <text>Device ID</text>
      <y>64</y>
      <width>180</width>
      <height>22</height>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
    </widget>
  </widget>
  <widget type="group" version="3.0.0">
    <name>HostResources</name>
    <x>12</x>
    <y>505</y>
    <width>1151</width>
    <height>175</height>
    <font>
      <font name="Header 3" family="Liberation Sans" style="BOLD" size="16.0">
      </font>
    </font>
    <widget type="label" version="2.0.0">
      <name>Title</name>
      <text>Host resources (Linux, 1 s poll)</text>
      <width>350</width>
      <height>24</height>
    </widget>
    <widget type="textupdate" version="2.0.0">
      <name>CPUUsage</name>
      <pv_name>$(P):ZynqMP:CPU:Usage</pv_name>
      <x>10</x>
      <y>30</y>
      <width>160</width>
      <height>32</height>
      <format>1</format>
      <precision>2</precision>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
      <vertical_alignment>1</vertical_alignment>
      <tooltip>$(P):ZynqMP:CPU:Usage (CPU_USAGE)

Busy percentage of all CPU cores together, computed by the monitor driver
from /proc/stat deltas over the 1 s poll interval. The first read reports 0
because there is no previous window yet. Minor alarm at 90%: sustained high
CPU load together with a high load average usually means the IOC is
oversubscribed.</tooltip>
      <border_alarm_sensitive>false</border_alarm_sensitive>
    </widget>
    <widget type="textupdate" version="2.0.0">
      <name>Load1</name>
      <pv_name>$(P):ZynqMP:CPU:Load1</pv_name>
      <x>180</x>
      <y>30</y>
      <width>160</width>
      <height>32</height>
      <format>1</format>
      <precision>2</precision>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
      <vertical_alignment>1</vertical_alignment>
      <tooltip>$(P):ZynqMP:CPU:Load1 (CPU_LOAD1)

1-minute load average from /proc/loadavg. Values around the core count mean
the CPUs are saturated; minor alarm at 4.0 (quad-core PS).</tooltip>
      <border_alarm_sensitive>false</border_alarm_sensitive>
    </widget>
    <widget type="textupdate" version="2.0.0">
      <name>Load5</name>
      <pv_name>$(P):ZynqMP:CPU:Load5</pv_name>
      <x>350</x>
      <y>30</y>
      <width>160</width>
      <height>32</height>
      <format>1</format>
      <precision>2</precision>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
      <vertical_alignment>1</vertical_alignment>
      <tooltip>$(P):ZynqMP:CPU:Load5 (CPU_LOAD5)

5-minute load average from /proc/loadavg. Smoother than the 1-minute value;
use it to judge a sustained load. Minor alarm at 4.0.</tooltip>
      <border_alarm_sensitive>false</border_alarm_sensitive>
    </widget>
    <widget type="textupdate" version="2.0.0">
      <name>Load15</name>
      <pv_name>$(P):ZynqMP:CPU:Load15</pv_name>
      <x>520</x>
      <y>30</y>
      <width>160</width>
      <height>32</height>
      <format>1</format>
      <precision>2</precision>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
      <vertical_alignment>1</vertical_alignment>
      <tooltip>$(P):ZynqMP:CPU:Load15 (CPU_LOAD15)

15-minute load average from /proc/loadavg. The slowest of the three; a high
15-minute value means the load is not a transient spike. Minor alarm at
4.0.</tooltip>
      <border_alarm_sensitive>false</border_alarm_sensitive>
    </widget>
    <widget type="textupdate" version="2.0.0">
      <name>MEMUsedPct</name>
      <pv_name>$(P):ZynqMP:MEM:UsedPct</pv_name>
      <x>765</x>
      <y>30</y>
      <width>160</width>
      <height>32</height>
      <format>1</format>
      <precision>2</precision>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
      <vertical_alignment>1</vertical_alignment>
      <tooltip>$(P):ZynqMP:MEM:UsedPct (MEM_USED_PCT)

Memory usage in percent: (MemTotal - MemAvailable) / MemTotal from
/proc/meminfo, in MB via the Used and Total fields to the right. Minor alarm
at 90%.</tooltip>
      <border_alarm_sensitive>false</border_alarm_sensitive>
    </widget>
    <widget type="textupdate" version="2.0.0">
      <name>MEMUsed</name>
      <pv_name>$(P):ZynqMP:MEM:Used</pv_name>
      <x>935</x>
      <y>30</y>
      <width>160</width>
      <height>32</height>
      <format>1</format>
      <precision>0</precision>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
      <vertical_alignment>1</vertical_alignment>
      <tooltip>$(P):ZynqMP:MEM:Used (MEM_USED)

Memory in use in MB: MemTotal - MemAvailable from /proc/meminfo. This is the
value the percentage on the left is computed from.</tooltip>
      <border_alarm_sensitive>false</border_alarm_sensitive>
    </widget>
    <widget type="textupdate" version="2.0.0">
      <name>MEMTotal</name>
      <pv_name>$(P):ZynqMP:MEM:Total</pv_name>
      <x>935</x>
      <y>90</y>
      <width>160</width>
      <height>32</height>
      <format>1</format>
      <precision>0</precision>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
      <vertical_alignment>1</vertical_alignment>
      <tooltip>$(P):ZynqMP:MEM:Total (MEM_TOTAL)

Total physical memory in MB (MemTotal from /proc/meminfo). Constant for a
given board; useful as the denominator of the used values.</tooltip>
      <border_alarm_sensitive>false</border_alarm_sensitive>
    </widget>
    <widget type="textupdate" version="2.0.0">
      <name>BootUsedPct</name>
      <pv_name>$(P):ZynqMP:DISK:Boot:UsedPct</pv_name>
      <x>10</x>
      <y>90</y>
      <width>160</width>
      <height>32</height>
      <format>1</format>
      <precision>2</precision>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
      <vertical_alignment>1</vertical_alignment>
      <tooltip>$(P):ZynqMP:DISK:Boot:UsedPct (DISK_USED_PCT /boot)

Used percentage of /boot, the FAT boot partition (SD card partition 1). The
boot files live here, so filling it up blocks firmware updates. Minor alarm
at 90%, major at 95%.</tooltip>
      <border_alarm_sensitive>false</border_alarm_sensitive>
    </widget>
    <widget type="textupdate" version="2.0.0">
      <name>BootFree</name>
      <pv_name>$(P):ZynqMP:DISK:Boot:Free</pv_name>
      <x>180</x>
      <y>90</y>
      <width>160</width>
      <height>32</height>
      <format>1</format>
      <precision>0</precision>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
      <vertical_alignment>1</vertical_alignment>
      <tooltip>$(P):ZynqMP:DISK:Boot:Free (DISK_FREE_MB /boot)

Free space of /boot in MB (space available to unprivileged users, as df
reports it). Informational; the alarm is on the percentage field.</tooltip>
      <border_alarm_sensitive>false</border_alarm_sensitive>
    </widget>
    <widget type="textupdate" version="2.0.0">
      <name>RootUsedPct</name>
      <pv_name>$(P):ZynqMP:DISK:Root:UsedPct</pv_name>
      <x>350</x>
      <y>90</y>
      <width>160</width>
      <height>32</height>
      <format>1</format>
      <precision>2</precision>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
      <vertical_alignment>1</vertical_alignment>
      <tooltip>$(P):ZynqMP:DISK:Root:UsedPct (DISK_USED_PCT /)

Used percentage of /, the root filesystem (SD card partition 2). Logs and
the IOC installation live here. Minor alarm at 90%, major at 95%.</tooltip>
      <border_alarm_sensitive>false</border_alarm_sensitive>
    </widget>
    <widget type="textupdate" version="2.0.0">
      <name>RootFree</name>
      <pv_name>$(P):ZynqMP:DISK:Root:Free</pv_name>
      <x>520</x>
      <y>90</y>
      <width>160</width>
      <height>32</height>
      <format>1</format>
      <precision>0</precision>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
      <vertical_alignment>1</vertical_alignment>
      <tooltip>$(P):ZynqMP:DISK:Root:Free (DISK_FREE_MB /)

Free space of the root filesystem in MB (space available to unprivileged
users, as df reports it). Informational; the alarm is on the percentage
field.</tooltip>
      <border_alarm_sensitive>false</border_alarm_sensitive>
    </widget>
    <widget type="label" version="2.0.0">
      <name>CPUUsageLabel</name>
      <text>CPU usage (%)</text>
      <x>10</x>
      <y>62</y>
      <width>160</width>
      <height>22</height>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
    </widget>
    <widget type="label" version="2.0.0">
      <name>Load1Label</name>
      <text>Load 1 min</text>
      <x>180</x>
      <y>62</y>
      <width>160</width>
      <height>22</height>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
    </widget>
    <widget type="label" version="2.0.0">
      <name>Load5Label</name>
      <text>Load 5 min</text>
      <x>350</x>
      <y>62</y>
      <width>160</width>
      <height>22</height>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
    </widget>
    <widget type="label" version="2.0.0">
      <name>Load15Label</name>
      <text>Load 15 min</text>
      <x>520</x>
      <y>62</y>
      <width>160</width>
      <height>22</height>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
    </widget>
    <widget type="label" version="2.0.0">
      <name>MEMUsedPctLabel</name>
      <text>Memory used (%)</text>
      <x>765</x>
      <y>62</y>
      <width>160</width>
      <height>22</height>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
    </widget>
    <widget type="label" version="2.0.0">
      <name>MEMUsedLabel</name>
      <text>Memory used (MB)</text>
      <x>935</x>
      <y>62</y>
      <width>160</width>
      <height>22</height>
      <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
    </widget>
    <widget type="label" version="2.0.0">
      <name>MEMTotalLabel</name>
      <text>Memory total (MB)</text>
      <x>935</x>
